"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nIt is close to a self-confessing novel that breaks away from the conventions of the mystery novel genre.  \nThe main characters of the work are aware that they are characters in a novel.  \nThey constantly break the  and complain about the author's laziness, trite plots, and old clichés they are forced to act out.  \n\nIf the author's early works focused on puzzle-solving, they tended to focus on human relationships and criminal motives after this work.  \nThis work seems to be a public manifesto for his literary direction.  \n\nThe detective who reluctantly acts like a genius and the character who plays the role of a self-aware fool are impressive."